course: dictionary
lesson: 11426
title: Acquiring: getting something new
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Acquiring means getting or gaining something. You can acquire things by buying them, or by learning them over time. «Sam is acquiring new skills by practising the piano every day.» Here acquiring means Sam is slowly learning new skills.
explain: In everyday talk you could say getting, buying or picking up instead. Try the swap: «Sam is getting new skills by practising the piano every day.» It still makes sense, so getting is a good everyday word for acquiring.
explain: Acquiring is a formal word, so you will meet it in exams and in serious writing. «The museum acquired a painting from a famous artist.» The clue is that a museum does not make its paintings, it gets them.
fact: Acquiring means getting or gaining something.
fact: Acquiring can mean buying something, as in «acquiring land».
fact: Acquiring a skill or habit means learning it over time, little by little.
fact: In science, a body can acquire immunity, which means it gains protection against an illness.
fact: Acquire, acquires, acquired and acquiring are all forms of the same word.
exercise: «She is acquiring a taste for spicy food.» What does acquiring mean here? | Losing something quickly | *Getting or gaining something over time | Breaking something into pieces | Throwing something away
exercise: Which everyday word could replace acquiring in «The shop is acquiring more stock»? | sleeping | hiding | *getting | forgetting
exercise: «Over many years, the bear acquired thick fur to keep warm.» What did the bear gain over time? | *thick fur | a long sleep | a wide river | a cold winter
test: «The club is acquiring a new field for its matches.» What does acquiring mean? | Cutting something down | *Getting or gaining something | Painting something | Leaving something behind
test: Which everyday word can replace acquiring? | losing | hiding | *picking up | forgetting
exam: «Maya spent a year acquiring the habit of reading every night.» What does acquiring mean here? | Breaking something | *Gaining something over time by learning | Dropping something | Forgetting something quickly
